# Citation metadata for the scoRch.simulator R package (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# GitHub and Zenodo use this root citation file.
# The software release and the study's processed dataset are separate records:
#   software: this repository, version 1.0.1, pending release
#             (the Zenodo software DOI is added under `identifiers:` once the
#             release is archived; see README.md)
#   dataset:  "Processed ERA5 daily maximum temperature data for SCORCH,
#             1940-2025", version 1.0.0, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21717874
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use scoRch.simulator, please cite the software and the study it
  implements. When you use the study's results, also cite the processed
  dataset (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21717874).
type: software
title: "scoRch.simulator: Spatiotemporal Classification of Regional Compound Heatwaves"
version: 1.0.1
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/fawazbouhamad/scoRch.simulator"
url: "https://github.com/fawazbouhamad/scoRch.simulator"
abstract: >-
  R implementation of SCORCH, the method of Bouhamad and Najibi for finding
  regionally extensive compound heatwaves in gridded daily maximum
  temperature: local heatwaves at every grid cell, regionally extensive days,
  DBSCAN clusters of heatwave cells, principal-component ellipses with
  temperature-weighted centroids, four compound-event types, and power-law
  and log-Gaussian Cox process models. One configuration file and one
  function run the complete workflow on ERA5 or prepared daily maximum
  temperature; the package reproduces the catalogs, tables and model results
  of the study from its archived input.
  The software archive is separate from the study's processed input dataset
  (version 1.0.0, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21717874) and the research record at
  https://github.com/fawazbouhamad/compound-heatwave-typologies-SCORCH.
  The bundled ERA5-derived US example contains 1,800 one-degree cells between
  25-50 N and 130-58 W for April-September 1996-2025, prepared from ARCO-ERA5.
  The authors' processing of the example, and their
  workflow and typology artwork, are CC BY 4.0. The inputs contain modified
  Copernicus Climate Change Service information 2026; original data terms
  and attribution are retained in demo/PROVENANCE.txt.
keywords:
  - compound heatwaves
  - heatwave typology
  - spatiotemporal clustering
  - DBSCAN
  - principal-component ellipses
  - log-Gaussian Cox process
  - ERA5 reanalysis
  - R package
authors:
